1 Introduction
However, newly added RVQ tokens carry no biological semantics, creating a cold-start problem for this LLM-native integration scheme. To address this, we develop Evidence-First Feature-Injection SFT , which provides the topK expressed genes alongside RVQ tokens and enforces an evidencebefore-conclusion reasoning structure. This design forces the language modeling loss to align RVQ hidden states with gene-level semantics, using the training objective itself as an implicit alignment available under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning signal. To further improve reasoning quality beyond what supervised learning alone can achieve, we construct a multi-component RLVR reward system [32] that evaluates answer accuracy via an LLM-as-judge approach [33] and assesses reasoning quality across five dimensions (independence, robustness, causality, granularity, and consistency). Furthermore, a Fact Verification module decomposes reasoning chains into atomic biological claims and validates each against the actual expression data of the cell. This expression-grounded verification extends the RLVR paradigm recently explored for biological reasoning [34] by providing fine-grained, cell-specific reward signals.
We evaluate RVQ-Alpha on a held-out pool of eight evaluation corpora curated from the ScBank singlecell collection (11,914 single-cell test samples spanning 12 tissues, five diseases, and 106 cell types), using disjoint training, validation, and test partitions (§4.5). Compared to supervised fine-tuning baselines, RLVR training yields consistent improvements: accuracy on the held-out immune-cell evaluation set improves from 70.7% to 82.8% (+12.1 pp), rare neuroblastoma recognition rises from 36.1% to 65.0% (+28.9 pp), population-level cell-type classification accuracy increases from 16.8% to 78.0% (+61.2 pp), and COVID-19 disease-state prediction accuracy doubles to 66.2%.
