E.4 FEW-SHOT CELL TYPE ANNOTATION
Table 6: Dataset statistics of few-shot annotation

Brain, threshold = 3000. Brain, # pre-train cell types = 6. Brain, # pre-train cells = 43750. Liver, threshold = 600. Liver, # pre-train cell types = 5. Liver, # pre-train cells = 4613. HLCA, threshold = 1000. HLCA, # pre-train cell types = 14. HLCA, # pre-train cells = 49279. Immune, threshold = 1000. Immune, # pre-train cell types = 14. Immune, # pre-train cells = 16969
We summarize the statistics of the datasets used in few-shot cell type annotation in Table 6. Under the few-shot setting, we pre-train scDiff for 1000 epochs with the base learning rate as 1 e -8 . Then we fine-tune scDiff on the few-shot set for 50 epochs, as mentioned in Section 4.2.1.
We illustrate the macro F1 score of one-shot cell type annotation in Fig. 5. Note that there is a significant drop of performance when the number of cell types reaches 5 in Liver dataset. The fifth cell type is B cell, and we observe that plasma cell have been included as the third cell type. Since B cells and plasma cells are from lymphocyte of B lineage (Hoffman et al., 2016), their gene expression levels are similar to each other. In the one-shot setting, two cells from different cell types share similar expression measurements, which will obscure the model. This observation reveals one drawback of diffusion classifier: it becomes increasingly challenging for diffusion classifier when the distributions of different classes are indistinguishable.
Figure 5: Macro F1 score of one-shot cell type annotation.

SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

Multi-panel line plot comparing F1 score across number of cell types. Four panels are labeled Brain, Liver, HLCA, and Immune. The x-axis is “Number of cell types” with values roughly 2–8, and the y-axis is “F1 score” from about 0.25 to 1.00. Legend shows three methods: CellTypist in blue, scDiff-Class in orange, and scDiff-LLM in green.

Main visible finding: scDiff-LLM generally has the highest or near-highest F1 scores across datasets, especially in Immune and HLCA, while CellTypist performs much lower in Immune as cell types increase. Performance generally declines with more cell types in Brain and Immune.
We summarize the macro accuracy score of few-shot cell type annotation in Fig. 6 and macro F1 score in Fig. 7. We fix the number of target cell type to 5 . In Brain and Immune, LLM-guided scDiff shows significant performance gain against class-guided scDiff . In the other two datasets, the two variants of scDiff achieve comaprable performance.
